        "title": "How helicopters work",
        "text": "If you have ever flown in a helicopter, you know that it is an exciting experience. Helicopters can fly almost anywhere, because they are the most versatile flying machines in existence today. While nearly all planes can only fly forward, a helicopter can also fly backward and sideways. The main feature of a helicopter, of course, is that it can hover over one point and, at the same time, either stay motionless in the air or rotate on its axis, which gives the pilot a panoramic view of the ground below. A car or a plane, on the other hand, can only change direction if it is moving.\n\nA helicopter can do a number of interesting tricks, such as rotating through 360 degrees while it travels along a straight line over the ground. A helicopter that is flying forward can also stop in mid-air and start hovering very quickly. Manoeuvres like these are impossible in a plane, which must fly forward at all times for its wings to provide lift. The extra freedom that helicopters offer is what makes them so exciting, but it also makes them complex.\n\nThe helicopter is controlled from the cockpit, the small area at the front where the pilot sits and looks out. The controls have to be within reach of the pilot's hands and feet, because they include pedals.\n\nThe word helicopter comes from Greek. Heliko- (from helix) means a spiral, and pteron means a wing. For the machine to be able to fly upward, the wings have to be moving, pushing air downward. It is this downward movement that creates the lift which raises the helicopter.\n\nThe easiest way to keep wings moving all the time is to rotate them. This can be done by fixing two or more wings on a drive shaft and spinning the shaft, rather like the blades of a ceiling fan. The rotating wings of a helicopter are generally narrow and thin because they have to spin so quickly. The helicopter's set of rotating wings is usually called the main rotor. If the wings are at a slight angle and the shaft spins, they start to push the air down and lift the helicopter. The main rotor is the most important part of the machine. As well as lifting it, the rotor allows the helicopter to move sideways, make turns and change height.\n\nTo spin the shaft with enough force to lift a human being and the helicopter, you need an engine. The engine's drive shaft is connected to the shaft of the main rotor. This arrangement works very well until the machine leaves the ground. At that moment, there is nothing to stop the engine (and so the body of the helicopter) from spinning in the opposite direction to the main rotor.\n\nTo stop the body from spinning, you need to apply a force to it. This is usually done by fixing another set of rotating wings to the tail boom, the part at the back of the helicopter that sticks out from the main body. These wings are known as the tail rotor. The tail rotor pushes the air sideways, working against the engine's tendency to spin the body, and so it stops the body of the helicopter from spinning.\n\nThe correct way to land a helicopter is to touch down evenly, with both landing skids touching the ground at all points at the same time. Otherwise there is a risk of severe vibration, which can cause serious damage and may even destroy the helicopter."
